flickos-control (1.14) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Updates: leave Check for Updates Now… out while gpk-update-viewer
    (gnome-package-updater) isn't installed instead of greying it out
    (HIG WIN-9); it appears once installed.
  * History page (Settings > History) and flickos-control history
    list|undo|clear: the changes to the desktop's settings, newest
    first, each with Undo while it is the setting's newest change. Every
    set records its change in ~/.local/state/flickos/history.jsonl;
    history.enabled (on by default, /etc/xdg/flickos/history.conf) turns
    recording off and clears it.
  * Depend on flickos-layouts (>= 1.22) and flick-tiler (>= 1.5), which
    record their changes and take default.
  * History: software changes from apt's log (installs, removals,
    updates; not the ones made while installing the system), each
    install or removal with Undo. control-helper packages-undo ID
    (polkit action org.flickos.control.packages-undo) checks the undo
    with apt-get -s first and refuses one that would remove another
    package or anything flickos-desktop depends on; packages-list and
    packages-plan only read and run without root.
  * History also records system settings through the new flickos-control
    system KEY [VALUE] (power button and lid, firewall, automatic login,
    automatic updates, time zone, network time, the login screen's
    keyboard layout), which the pages now run; default applications
    through the new default-app list|set; startup applications; and user
    accounts and added languages (shown without Undo, except
    administrator on or off).
  * History: put the settings back as they were (Put Back as It Was…,
    flickos-control history restore WHEN [--dry-run]): every setting
    changed since then that needs no password gets its value then, shown
    first, recorded as one change whose Undo puts them back. System
    settings and software keep their own Undo.

 -- Jon <dvbondoy@gmail.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 07:20:45 +0800

